 "authorLine": "Agent run, recognition of governments that took power by force in Latin America, 2026-10-25",
 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: for most of the record the test was whether a new government controlled the country, had the people's acquiescence and would meet its international obligations (stated in 1900 and again in 1948). The series records three departures: the Wilson administration's demand from 1913, in Mexico and Central America though not in Peru, that governments set up by force be constitutionally elected, the Central American treaty rule of 1923–1936, and wartime recognition by inter-American consultation from December 1943. Before recognizing, Washington asked for concrete undertakings: frontier guarantees from Díaz (1877), Huerta's withdrawal from the election (1913), a customs convention and four more undertakings from Haiti (1914), a property-protecting treaty from Mexico (1921), proof of no Axis inspiration (1944–1945), and from 1962 election dates and civil liberties. The set is about 2,300 documents in 137 of the 553 volumes in this copy of the index (the whole series as the app lists it), about 1,200 of them in the editors' 51 recognition chapters (1895–1950). Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed documents came overwhelmingly from the State Department's Central Decimal File (Record Group 59), chiefly each country's political-affairs and government files, then from the Subject-Numeric File (1963–1973), the Central Foreign Policy File, Department lot files and presidential libraries. The editors' unprinted citations point back to the same country files, to two Bureau of Inter-American Affairs lot files and to Carter Library papers. What this request cannot get from FRUS: a full run of post-1950 cases (no editors' recognition headings after 1950), the nineteenth-century files behind the despatches (no source notes before 1906), the Latin American governments' own deliberations, and the 1980s (one candidate document in the two 1981–1988 volumes that concern Latin America). The question's own phrases (came to power, by force, Latin America) are nearly absent from the documents.",
